WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
Seabiscuit

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:04.230
It was the height of the depression and America
was in desperate need of a hero.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:08.490
They found it in the most unlikely of places
. . . a battler who looked about as beat up

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:09.840
as the nation itself.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.920
For a racehorse, he didn’t have much going
for him.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.420
His legs were too short, his neck was too
thick and he had a swaying foreleg that jutted

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:17.420
out wildly.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.790
Worst of all, he had a mind of his own.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:23.170
When he felt like running he’d run, when
he didn’t, he wouldn’t.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:28.890
It’s hard to believe that this horse, having
lost his first 17 starts, would become a legend

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.390
– the greatest racehorse in history.

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:37.070
In this episode of Biographics we reveal the
amazing story of Seabiscuit – the horse

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:46.510
that brought a nation together.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:47.510
* * *

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:55.110
Pedigree

00:00:55.110 --> 00:01:00.370
Seabiscuit was born (or more accurately ‘foaled’)
on May 23rd, 1933 in Lexington, Kentucky.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:05.700
He was the son of a nasty-tempered rogue named
Hard Tack, and a gentle mare named Swing On.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.240
Seabiscuit’s father had great speed, no
doubt owing to his own pedigree.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:13.500
His father, Man o’ War, was considered by
most experts at the time to have been the

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.210
greatest racehorse ever produced…

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.530
although his grandson would certainly go on
to challenge that claim.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.369
The problem with Hard Tack was that he was
uncontrollable...

00:01:22.369 --> 00:01:25.940
Even hall of fame trainer ‘Sunny’ Jim
Fitzsimmons couldn’t tame him.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.560
Seabiscuit’s mother was the complete opposite
of Hard Tack.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.830
She was a calm, gentle horse with thick knees
and short legs.

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.780
Swing On wasn’t very fast though, so she
never raced...

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.450
The colt that she sired didn’t look very
promising.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:41.020
His legs were stubby, his knees were thick
and he looked sort of ‘funny’ when he

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.020
ran.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.619
Even Seabiscuit’s color was against him.

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:48.720
He was a solid muddy bay with no distinguishing
white stripe or star on his face.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.920
These horses were all owned by Ogden Mills,
Secretary of the U.S. Treasury and his sister,

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:55.970
Mrs H.C, Phibbs.

00:01:55.970 --> 00:02:00.310
Their racing stable was one of the finest
in America, with no expense being spared in

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:02.090
the care and training of the horses.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:07.829
However, Mrs Phibbs insisted that only horses
that would bring honor to the stable be kept.

00:02:07.829 --> 00:02:12.810
The only reason that Hard Tack and Swing On
were still around was that they both had strong

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:13.810
pedigrees.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:17.250
It was hoped that their offspring would have
amazing speed…

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:21.540
During his first year, Seabiscuit, along with
his mother, was put into a 50-acre pasture

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.560
with nine other thoroughbred mares and their
newborn foals.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.930
In every group of colts there are usually
two that stand out – one is the hero, the

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:34.670
better looking, more graceful, and faster
one; the other is the outcast, an awkward,

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:39.160
looking runty little fellow who looked as
if he slipped in when no one was watching.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:43.379
In this group the hero was Granville, and
the outcast was Seabiscuit.

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:47.090
But, something in Seabiscuit stood out to
keen observers.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:51.060
Several times a day the colts would race up
and down the paddock with Seabiscuit always

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.060
at the rear.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.080
Yet, the gangly colt was never discouraged.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.720
The farther he was left behind, the harder
he drove himself – and the longer the race,

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.530
the nearer he came to catching up.

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:04.500
With his big head and short spindly legs,
Seabiscuit spent more energy than the other

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:05.599
colts in running.

00:03:05.599 --> 00:03:08.080
As a result, he had a larger appetite than
the other colts.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:13.750
He developed stronger and tougher leg muscles,
and by late fall he was no longer running

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:15.300
at the back of the pack…

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:20.500
Emerging as a yearling on January 1st, 1934,
Seabiscuit looked more like a ragged range

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:21.670
pony than a thoroughbred.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:25.750
He had failed to shed his winter coat and
his legs and back were a few inches shorter

00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:26.750
than average.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:30.240
In fact, he was such an ‘ugly duckling’
that when Mrs Phibbs came in April to inspect

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.570
her horses, Seabiscuit was hidden away in
an outlying barn.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:36.990
By now, though, Seabiscuit had found his running
legs.

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:38.880
He was challenging Granville for the lead...

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:43.879
He may not have had the physical attributes
of the other horses, but he was full of stamina

00:03:43.879 --> 00:03:45.400
and determination.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:48.420
By mid-summer the rivals were racing neck
and neck.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:53.310
It was then that Seabiscuit developed a habit
that would be a trademark for the rest of

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:57.090
his life – whenever they were vying for
the lead, he would snort at Granville, as

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:05.500
if attempting to bully him and throw him off
pace.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:06.980
Training

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:15.980
In June, 1934 Granville and Seabiscuit were
shipped to ‘Sunny’ Jim Fitzsimmons, recognized

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:18.329
as the finest trainer in the country.

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:23.020
Fitzsimmons had developed a hatred for Seabiscuit’s
father, Hard Tack, after years of being unable

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:24.619
to break him of his vicious habits.

00:04:24.619 --> 00:04:28.759
He had also given up on Swing On, finding
her too slow and lazy.

00:04:28.759 --> 00:04:33.289
This did not bode well for Seabiscuit, who
Fitzsimmons looked upon as a curse from the

00:04:33.289 --> 00:04:34.349
very beginning...

00:04:34.349 --> 00:04:39.330
He put the majority of his energies into training
Granville, while forcing Seabiscuit to conform.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:44.099
Seabiscuit, meanwhile, was determined not
to comply - he simply refused to go fast!

00:04:44.099 --> 00:04:49.699
Finally, an exasperated Fitzsimmons gave his
jockey a wooden stake and told him to ‘hit

00:04:49.699 --> 00:04:52.229
Seabiscuit just as many times as you can in
a quarter mile.’

00:04:52.229 --> 00:04:55.610
They ran him against Faust, the fastest sprinter
among the group.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:05:01.340
In trying to escape the severe beating, Seabiscuit
exploded around the track, leaving Faust far

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:02.340
in his wake.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:05.319
He ran the quarter mile in 22 seconds and
2/5ths.

00:05:05.319 --> 00:05:08.839
If you didn’t know this is a pretty incredible
time…

00:05:08.839 --> 00:05:12.860
It amazed Fitzsimmons, but it also confirmed
in his mind that Seabiscuit would only show

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:15.159
his potential under severe punishment.

00:05:15.159 --> 00:05:20.539
When, on January 1st 1935, the horses under
Fitzsimmons became two-year-olds, Seabiscuit

00:05:20.539 --> 00:05:21.539
was tossed aside.

00:05:21.539 --> 00:05:23.839
He was put to work as a hack racer.

00:05:23.839 --> 00:05:28.169
On January 19th he was entered into a short
race, losing by four and a half lengths...

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:33.830
During 1935, he ran a total of 35 races, losing
the first seventeen.

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:35.659
Not a great start…

00:05:35.659 --> 00:05:40.669
It was only when his jockey stopped beating
him and let him run his own race that he showed

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:41.830
real promise.

00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:45.099
He won five races, setting three track records
in the process.

00:05:45.099 --> 00:05:50.919
Still, Fitzsimmons was desperate to get rid
of him, convinced that Seabiscuit was an inconsistent

00:05:50.919 --> 00:05:51.919
no hoper.

00:05:51.919 --> 00:05:57.939
On August 3rd 1936, Fitzsimmons entered Seabiscuit
in the $6000 Claiming Stakes at Saratoga.

00:05:57.939 --> 00:06:03.059
Fortunately for him, this just happened to
be one of those days when Seabiscuit decided

00:06:03.059 --> 00:06:07.009
to turn it on – he won by six full lengths.

00:06:07.009 --> 00:06:12.740
Up in the stands an old time cowboy and veteran
trainer, Tom Smith, saw heart and courage

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:14.229
in Seabiscuit.

00:06:14.229 --> 00:06:16.589
He decided that he had to have him.

00:06:16.589 --> 00:06:21.729
A week later Seabiscuit was purchased by Charles
Howard for $8000 and handed over to Tom Smith

00:06:21.729 --> 00:06:22.729
for training.

00:06:22.729 --> 00:06:27.620
The horse finally had a trainer who both respected
him and saw his limitless potential.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:30.689
Seabiscuit was subjected to an intense conditioning
campaign.

00:06:30.689 --> 00:06:36.669
The horse that no one had wanted was pampered
and groomed with a focus on foot care to recover

00:06:36.669 --> 00:06:40.840
from the gruelling schedule he had been forced
to endure under Fitzsimmons.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:44.939
As a result of the care and attention that
was lavished upon him, Seabiscuit began performing

00:06:44.939 --> 00:06:47.179
more consistently on the practice track.

00:06:47.179 --> 00:06:50.419
He became gentler and seemed to develop a
competitive spirit.

00:06:50.419 --> 00:06:54.689
He also developed a huge appetite, eating
anything that was put before him.

00:06:54.689 --> 00:06:59.189
To keep his weight down, grooms were instructed
to muzzle him at night to prevent him from

00:06:59.189 --> 00:07:00.349
eating his straw bedding.

00:07:00.349 --> 00:07:05.849
A combination of kindness, patience and the
skilful training of Tom Smith managed to transform

00:07:05.849 --> 00:07:09.839
Seabiscuit’s courage and intelligence into
raw, unbridled speed.

00:07:09.839 --> 00:07:13.349
All that was needed now was to match Seabiscuit
with the right jockey...

00:07:13.349 --> 00:07:14.349
Racing

00:07:14.349 --> 00:07:18.610
Smith found him in the form of Jack ‘Red’
Pollard, a feisty 27-year-old Canadian, who’d

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:22.199
been roaming around the west riding and boxing
for over a decade.

00:07:22.199 --> 00:07:24.499
His love for thoroughbreds was as deep as
Tom Smith’s...

00:07:24.499 --> 00:07:28.679
Yet, although he was one of the better jockeys
on the bush circuit, he’d won only three

00:07:28.679 --> 00:07:30.919
stake races in his life.

00:07:30.919 --> 00:07:35.240
When Red first entered Seabiscuit’s stall,
the horse was busy munching on straw.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:39.089
Seabiscuit looked up, gave a snort and then
ambled over to Pollard and rubbed his muzzle

00:07:39.089 --> 00:07:40.800
against Red’s shoulder.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.250
And with that, one of history’s great partnerships
was born.

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:49.629
Satisfied that he was ready, Howard entered
Seabiscuit into a series of races in Detroit.

00:07:49.629 --> 00:07:55.199
In two races, the Detroit Governor’s Handicap
and the Scarsdale Handicap, he came home first,

00:07:55.199 --> 00:07:58.379
netting $12,900 in prize money for his owner.

00:07:58.379 --> 00:08:03.479
Over the next two months the winnings accrued
to a remarkable $40,000.

00:08:03.479 --> 00:08:06.689
A delighted Howard gave him a two-month vacation...\

00:08:06.689 --> 00:08:11.469
In November, 1936, Seabiscuit was sent by
rail to California to compete in his last

00:08:11.469 --> 00:08:12.969
two races as a three-year-old.

00:08:12.969 --> 00:08:15.289
The first was the Bay Bridge Handicap.

00:08:15.289 --> 00:08:19.879
Despite starting badly, Red and Seabiscuit
soon found their stride and they won by five

00:08:19.879 --> 00:08:20.879
lengths.

00:08:20.879 --> 00:08:24.649
In their last race of the year, the World’s
Fair Handicap, they led from the start, cruising

00:08:24.649 --> 00:08:26.110
to an easy victory.

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:30.899
By the beginning of 1937, Seabiscuit was beginning
to develop a reputation as an up and coming

00:08:30.899 --> 00:08:31.899
champion.

00:08:31.899 --> 00:08:36.729
The focus in the early part of the year was
March’s Santa Anita Cup - the biggest race

00:08:36.729 --> 00:08:38.500
on the West Coast.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:42.529
Seabiscuit started the year well, winning
a warm up race at Santa Anita in January.

00:08:42.529 --> 00:08:47.110
Then, in the San Antonio Handicap, he was
bumped at the start and pushed wide, finishing

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:48.990
a disappointing fifth.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:54.339
Now it was time for the big one – the $125,000
Santa Anita Cup.

00:08:54.339 --> 00:08:58.639
Coming down the home straight, Seabiscuit
was a half-length ahead of Rosemont, the horse

00:08:58.639 --> 00:09:00.510
which had won the week before at San Antonio.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:05.180
But Red Pollard, who was blind in his right
eye, didn’t know that Rosemont was closing

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:08.120
in and failed to urge Seabiscuit into the
next gear...

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:10.610
Rosemont won by a nose.

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:14.709
This disappointment though, was followed up
by three easy wins, establishing Seabiscuit’s

00:09:14.709 --> 00:09:18.709
reputation on the West Coast as the breakout
thoroughbred of the year.

00:09:18.709 --> 00:09:22.940
By this time Charles Howard was making some
pretty great money off of his investment.

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:24.570
But he wanted more.

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:30.220
He was determined that Seabiscuit be recognized
as the greatest race horse in the entire United

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:31.220
States.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:35.450
That meant breaking into the far more prestigious
East Coast circuit.

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:42.589
So, between June 26th and August 7th, he entered
Seabiscuit into five east coast races, and

00:09:42.589 --> 00:09:43.910
won every one of them.

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:49.690
He finished 1937 with a record of 11 wins
out of 15 races and was the biggest money

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:50.940
earner in the country.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:55.470
He was also now the most famous horse in America,
with the press having a field day with his

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:56.600
underdog story.

00:09:56.600 --> 00:10:01.189
The nation’s most prestigious equine award,
however, did not go to Seabiscuit that year.

00:10:01.189 --> 00:10:05.759
The winner of the American Horse of the Year
Award was the darling of the East Coast set,

00:10:05.759 --> 00:10:12.379
War Admiral, who had just won the coveted
Triple Crown.

00:10:12.379 --> 00:10:20.730
Rivalry with War Admiral

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:22.660
Interestingly, War Admiral and Seabiscuit
were actually blood relatives.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:27.220
They were the son and grandson of one of the
greatest thoroughbreds in history, Man o’

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:28.220
War.

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:30.380
Seabiscuit’s father, Hard Tack, was War
Admiral’s brother.

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:35.279
In 1937, War Admiral won every race he started,
including the Triple Crown.

00:10:35.279 --> 00:10:40.050
This prestigious honor is bestowed upon thoroughbreds
that win three major races in succession:

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:43.580
The Kentucky Derby, Preakness Stakes and Belmont
Stakes.

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.529
But War Admiral’s victory at Belmont…
it was bitter sweet.

00:10:46.529 --> 00:10:49.779
An injury prevented him from racing again
for several months.

00:10:49.779 --> 00:10:51.649
War Admiral was a natural competitor.

00:10:51.649 --> 00:10:55.269
The lively activity of race day seemed to
fill him with nervous excitement.

00:10:55.269 --> 00:10:58.680
He was so competitive it was difficult for
handlers to hold him back when the saddling

00:10:58.680 --> 00:10:59.680
bell rang.

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:02.990
His temperament was fiery and skittish - just
like his brother Hard Tack.

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:05.970
But this personality usually worked to his
advantage.

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:09.769
He never willingly walked into the gate and
once the starters got him in he would act

00:11:09.769 --> 00:11:11.640
up causing delays and false starts…

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:16.010
At the beginning of 1938, War Admiral was
the best horse in the east and Seabiscuit

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:18.220
was the best horse in the west.

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:20.750
Race fans were anxious for the two champions
to meet.

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:25.600
But Charles Howard knew that despite the public’s
fascination with his unlikely star, if Seabiscuit

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:29.470
were to be taken seriously as a challenger
to War Admiral, he would have to establish

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:31.519
his own reputation in the east.

00:11:31.519 --> 00:11:35.899
Howard was a master promoter and he capitalized
on the established racing rivalry between

00:11:35.899 --> 00:11:37.699
the east coast and the west coast.

00:11:37.699 --> 00:11:42.019
He was determined to prove that a horse coming
out of California could be the equal of anything

00:11:42.019 --> 00:11:43.370
that ran in the east.

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:47.970
He began issuing public challenges to Sam
Riddle, the owner of War Admiral.

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:52.589
Riddle ignored him, considering it demeaning
for his champion thoroughbred to meet a western

00:11:52.589 --> 00:11:54.120
horse...

00:11:54.120 --> 00:12:00.029
After months of incessant media chiding, though,
well, he finally agreed.

00:12:00.029 --> 00:12:08.191
Fastest Horse in the USA

00:12:08.191 --> 00:12:12.660
The question of who was the fastest horse
in the United States would be decided by way

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:16.750
of a race to be held at Pimlico Racetrack
in Baltimore, Maryland.

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:22.440
This would take place on November 1st, 1938.The
distance was one and three sixteenth miles.

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:27.740
Riddle insisted, however, that the race begin
with a no gate, or walk-up start, to avoid

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:30.089
War Admiral’s temperamental behaviour in
the gate.

00:12:30.089 --> 00:12:34.720
Howard agreed to start the race with a starting
bell, but he also had a condition; the track

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:37.019
had to be ‘fast’ or dry.

00:12:37.019 --> 00:12:38.870
Seabiscuit did not run well in the mud.

00:12:38.870 --> 00:12:42.320
If the track was too wet because of rain,
the race would be called off.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:44.930
Then things started to go wrong in the Seabiscuit
camp.

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:49.800
Red Pollard fractured his leg while working
with another horse and was unable to race.

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:55.509
Pollard’s good friend and fellow top jockey
George ‘The Iceman’ Woolf would ride Seabiscuit.

00:12:55.509 --> 00:13:00.079
Up until that time, every match race in history
had been won by the horse that was in the

00:13:00.079 --> 00:13:01.149
lead from the start.

00:13:01.149 --> 00:13:06.600
Throughout all of his races in 1937, War Admiral
had never let another horse in front of him.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:10.079
Yet, Seabiscuit was a ‘come from behind’
winner.

00:13:10.079 --> 00:13:14.689
War Admiral’s trainer George Conway and
jockey Charley Kurtsinger were confident that

00:13:14.689 --> 00:13:16.860
their horse was faster than Seabiscuit.

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:22.009
So, they concentrated on building War Admiral’s
stamina to endure the mile-long distance.

00:13:22.009 --> 00:13:25.199
Meanwhile, Tom Smith was concentrating on
the start of the race.

00:13:25.199 --> 00:13:28.870
He put together a homemade bell that started
like the starting bell at Pimlico.

00:13:28.870 --> 00:13:32.460
When he rang the bell, Woolf cracked the whip
on Seabiscuit’s backside.

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:35.589
This conditioned Seabsicuit to run at top
speed immediately.

00:13:35.589 --> 00:13:38.639
The race of the century was a worldwide sensation.

00:13:38.639 --> 00:13:43.220
The race track was so crowded that ten thousand
people crowded outside the gates, sitting

00:13:43.220 --> 00:13:47.160
in trees, and standing on cars roof tops to
try to see the race.

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:51.079
Across the United States 40 million people
tuned in to their radios.

00:13:51.079 --> 00:13:54.970
Many of them had never before cared about
horse racing but now were eager to hear history

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:56.370
being made.

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:59.000
Most of them were rooting for Seabiscuit.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:02.959
Charley Kurtsinger, on War Admiral, wore black
and yellow silks and rode the rail, while

00:14:02.959 --> 00:14:07.629
George Woolf, with red and white silks, sitting
astride Seabiscuit, took the outside.

00:14:07.629 --> 00:14:11.309
War Admiral prepared for the race the way
he always did, rearing up and tormenting his

00:14:11.309 --> 00:14:12.309
handlers.

00:14:12.309 --> 00:14:14.779
Meanwhile Seabiscuit calmly headed for the
post.

00:14:14.779 --> 00:14:16.759
The flag was raised and the horses lined up.

00:14:16.759 --> 00:14:20.370
But just as the starter was about to ring
the bell, Woolf pulled Seabiscuit back.

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:23.130
It was a tactic to make War Admiral nervous.

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:26.129
When they lined up a second time, Kurtsigner
pulled his horse back.

00:14:26.129 --> 00:14:29.730
On the third try, both horses lined up at
exactly the same moment.

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:31.879
The starter bell rang and they were off.

00:14:31.879 --> 00:14:34.379
The two thoroughbreds pounded down the track
neck and neck.

00:14:34.379 --> 00:14:37.209
It was commonly believed that War Admiral
would take the early lead.

00:14:37.209 --> 00:14:42.300
So, the crowd were astonished when Woolf cracked
the whip and Seabiscuit edged in front.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:44.550
Around the first turn Seabiscuit was still
ahead.

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:47.370
Woolf veered left and took the rail from Kurtsinger.

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:51.620
Then he slowed Seabiscuit right down in front
of War Admiral in order to conserve energy.

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:55.190
Kurtsinger now either had to slow down himself
or pass Woolf.

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:58.999
War Admiral drew closer but Seabiscuit was
still ahead by a length and a half.

00:14:58.999 --> 00:15:02.240
Going into the backstretch, the distance widened
to two lengths.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:07.209
Then Kurtsinger cracked the whip and in just
a few strides War Admiral was even with Seabiscuit.

00:15:07.209 --> 00:15:09.339
The crowd were going wild.

00:15:09.339 --> 00:15:13.180
The horses charged down the straight, neck
and neck.

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:17.089
Then War Admiral took the lead, but only for
a moment as Seabiscuit sped up.

00:15:17.089 --> 00:15:18.810
He was in front again...

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:21.079
Turning into the stretch it was anybody’s
race.

00:15:21.079 --> 00:15:23.209
They rounded the far turn and were headed
for home.

00:15:23.209 --> 00:15:27.079
They were coupled shoulder to shoulder, head
to head, eye to eye…

00:15:27.079 --> 00:15:31.329
Around the turn as they headed for home, Woolf
cracked the whip, asking Seabiscuit for everything

00:15:31.329 --> 00:15:32.329
he had.

00:15:32.329 --> 00:15:33.749
He loosened the reins and his horse answered.

00:15:33.749 --> 00:15:37.660
Dropping his chin, his ears pinned back he
raced toward the finish line.

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:43.759
The crowd were astounded to see Seabiscuit
break away by one, two, three lengths.

00:15:43.759 --> 00:15:46.160
In the end it was no contest.

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:49.939
Seabiscuit was the winner by four lengths;
leaving War Admiral in his dust.

00:15:49.939 --> 00:15:54.240
As Seabiscuit slowed to a gallop, thousands
of fans burst through the infield chasing

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:55.589
their underdog hero.

00:15:55.589 --> 00:15:58.580
In the winner’s circle Seabiscuit was donned
with a blanket of yellow flowers.

00:15:58.580 --> 00:16:03.680
War Admiral’s barn met their defeated champion,
and slipped away almost unnoticed...

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:07.709
Seabiscuit was now, not only the national
racing champion, but the fastest and most

00:16:07.709 --> 00:16:09.170
famous horse in the world.

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:12.490
He easily won horse of the year in 1938.

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:14.589
However, the joy for Seabiscuit was short
lived.

00:16:14.589 --> 00:16:17.080
In his very next race he ruptured a ligament
in his leg.

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:21.059
He spent most of 1939 healing at the Howard
ranch in California.

00:16:21.059 --> 00:16:24.860
The comeback for both Seabiscuit and jockey
Red Pollard, still recovering from his own

00:16:24.860 --> 00:16:28.649
leg injury, was to be the 1940 Santa Anita
Handicap.

00:16:28.649 --> 00:16:32.420
During the race Seabiscuit got caught in a
pocket, which Red tried desperately to get

00:16:32.420 --> 00:16:33.420
out of.

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:36.459
Finally finding his gap, he yelled, ‘Now,
pops!’

00:16:36.459 --> 00:16:46.819
Seabiscuit broke through, took the lead and
never looked back...

00:16:46.819 --> 00:16:49.680
Retirement

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:53.839
Following the Santa Anita victory, Seabiscuit
retired with a record of 33 victories from

00:16:53.839 --> 00:16:54.839
89 races.

00:16:54.839 --> 00:17:00.870
He had earned Charles Howard $438,000, an
American record at that time.

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:04.400
Seabiscuit died suddenly of a heart attack
on May 17th, 1947.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:08.990
Three years later, Charles Howard’s horse
Noor won the 1950 Santa Anita Handicap.

00:17:08.990 --> 00:17:12.390
When a reporter suggested that perhaps he’d
found another Seabiscuit, he answered . . .

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:32.129
‘Sir, there will never be another Seabiscuit.’

